<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">God Defend New Zealand</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">poem written in the 1870s; music <a href="/years/1876">1876</a>; Māori version <a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Aotearoa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;God Defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>&quot; began as a poem written in the 1870s by Thomas Bracken of Dunedin, an Irish-born immigrant raised in Victoria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. In 1876 The Saturday Advertiser held a competition to set it to music, judged by three prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> musicians, with a prize of ten guineas.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>God Defend New Zealand</b>&quot; began as a poem written in the 1870s by Thomas Bracken of Dunedin, an Irish-born immigrant raised in Victoria, Australia.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-defend-new-zealand">God Defend New Zealand</a></li></ul></section>
